Research complete: the canonical Exercism suite confirms the prompt's rules plus key edge cases — `y` is a consonant at word start ("yellow"→"ellowyay") but a vowel inside a cluster ("my"→"ymay"), and `qu` (possibly preceded by consonants) moves as one unit ("square"→"aresquay"), while a `qu` after the first vowel is irrelevant ("liquid"→"iquidlay").

**COV-3/§2 done — two approaches evaluated:** (A) left-to-right scan for the first vowel-ish letter, treating `u`-after-`q` as part of the onset, with `xr`/`yt`/vowel-start as rule 1; (B) regex extraction of the onset cluster. Chose (A): no regex imports, O(n), and it naturally handles all 4 rules' precedence. Rejected (B) as less readable for the `qu`-precedes-vowel subtlety.

