Done. Implementation and verification complete.

**What I implemented** — `pig_latin.py:1`: `translate(text)` splits the phrase on whitespace and maps each word through an inner `translate_word` that applies the 4 rules via a left-to-right scan for the first vowel-ish letter:
- Rule 1 (vowel / `xr` / `yt` start) → `word + "ay"`
- Rule 3 (`u` directly after `q`, with optional leading consonants — checked first) → onset incl. `qu` moves to end → `+ "ay"` (e.g. `square→aresquay`)
- Rule 2 (plain consonant cluster) → cluster to end → `+ "ay"`
- Rule 4 (`y` treated as a vowel only when it follows consonants, i.e. index > 0) → `my→ymay`, `rhythm→ythmrhay`, while `yellow→ellowyay`
- Fallback `+ "ay"` for all-consonant words

**How I verified it** — Ran `python3` importing the module against all 23 canonical Exercism cases (vowel starts, `xr`/`yt`, single/multi-consonant, `qu` variants incl. `queen`/`square`/`liquid`/`equal`, `y`-edge cases, full phrase `quick fast run`): **23/23 PASS**. `python3 -m py_compile pig_latin.py` → OK. No syntax/runtime errors. Only `pig_latin.py` was changed; no test files created/modified.

